s://kb.iu.edu/d/adyf\\n\\nFrom Chris Lorch:\\n<https://github.com/c21u/canvascrosslist>\\n<https://c21u.gatech.edu/>\\n\\nFrom Chris Lorch:\\nI’d bet that Yakut knows the folks at Georgia tech who work on this.\\n\\nFrom Chris Lorch:\\nClosest doc I could find that documents GT approach [link to a file]\\n\\nFrom Chris Lorch:\\nLooks like React\\n\\nFrom Chris Lorch:\\nIt looks like IU is considering 1.3 role style\\n<https://github.com/indiana-university/lms-canvas-crosslisting/pull/7/commits/ad2823f3f630d8b40e047835ef0b1c1b08da1ecb>\\n\\nFrom Chris Lorch:\\n(Those “purl” roles became an issue in Sakai 1.3 tools) ^\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How helpful is `response` as an answer to `prompt`?\", \"criteria\": [\"Not useful or helpful at all; completely missed the essence of what the user wanted.\", \"Borderline unhelpful: mostly does not capture what the user was looking for, but still usable and helpful in a small way.\", \"Partially helpful: misses the overall goal of the user's query in some way; did not fully satisfy what the user was looking for.\", \"Mostly helpful and mainly aligned with what the user was looking for, but there is still some room for improvement.\", \"Extremely helpful and completely aligned with the spirit of what the prompt was asking for.\"]}", "label": "2", "soft_label": null, "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"nvidia/HelpSteer2\", \"license\": \"cc-by-4.0\", \"domain\": \"llm-judging\", \"task_family\": \"response-quality\"}"}
{"id": "helpsteer2_helpfulness/test/205", "source": "helpsteer2_helpfulness", "primitive": "score", "split": "test", "state": "{\"prompt\": \"want you to act as my English (US) teacher and best friend. I will be the other person and you will ask me questions about my life and everything. Do not write all the conservation at once. Ask me the questions and wait for my answers. If I make any English (US) mistakes, I want you to correct me and explain the correction, like a professor of english. Give a clear explanation of each correction. Our conversation will start with Start.\\n Start: Please help me write a google apps script that does the following: a) Forward every new gmail email with a label \\\"forward\\\" to another email address. b) after the forward, remove the label \\\"forward\\\" and add a label \\\"completed.\\\"\\n<extra_id_1>Assistant\\nI'm sorry, I'm not sure I can do this. I have very little knowledge of google apps scripting. I've been mostly sailing and talking with ChatGPT. Can you give me more details about what you're trying to do?\\n<extra_id_1>User\\nsailing and talking with ChatGPT\\n \\n\\n Please write in English (US) language.\\n<extra_id_1>Assistant\\nSure. I'm happy to help. I think this should be pretty simple. The first step is to make a new google apps script. Here's how you do that:  \\n \\n\\n \\n\\n 1. Go to https://script.google.com\\n \\n\\n 2. Click \\\"New Script\\\" in the top-left corner of the screen\\n \\n\\n 3. In the \\\"Create a new script\\\" window that pops up, click the drop-down menu next to \\\"Create\\\" and select \\\"Google Apps Script\\\"\\n \\n\\n 4. Name the script \\\"GmailForwarder\\\"\\n<extra_id_1>User\\nThis was perfect. Well done!\\n \\n\\n Please write in English (US) language.\", \"response\": \"Thank you! I'm glad I could help. Is there anything else I can assist you with?\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How helpful is `response` as an answer to `prompt`?\", \"criteria\": [\"Not useful or helpful at all; completely missed the essence of what the user wanted.\", \"Borderline unhelpful: mostly does not capture what the user was looking for, but still usable and helpful in a small way.\", \"Partially helpful: misses the overall goal of the user's query in some way; did not fully satisfy what the user was looking for.\", \"Mostly helpful and mainly aligned with what the user was looking for, but there is still some room for improvement.\", \"Extremely helpful and completely aligned with the spirit of what the prompt was asking for.\"]}", "label": "4", "soft_label": null, "meta": "{\"hf_id\": \"nvidia/HelpSteer2\", \"license\": \"cc-by-4.0\", \"domain\": \"llm-judging\", \"task_family\": \"response-quality\"}"}
{"id": "helpsteer2_helpfulness/test/82", "source": "helpsteer2_helpfulness", "primitive": "score", "split": "test", "state": "{\"prompt\": \"Make up the synopsis for 5 new Seinfeld episodes\", \"response\": \"Sure, here are the synopses for five new Seinfeld episodes:\\n\\n1. \\\"The Parking Garage\\\": Jerry, Kramer, and George are stuck in a parking garage after hours, unable to find their cars in the massive garage. They search for hours, with no luck, and have to spend the night in the garage.\\n\\n2. \\\"The Potluck\\\": Jerry is invited to a potluck dinner with his neighbors, but he doesn't know what to bring. He decides to bring a dish that he knows everyone will love, but it turns out to be a disaster.\\n\\n3. \\\"The Haircut\\\": George gets a haircut from a new stylist, but he's unhappy with the results. He tries to get a refund, but the salon refuses. So, he decides to get revenge by writing a negative review online.\\n\\n4. \\\"The Dog Walker\\\": Kramer gets a job as a dog walker, but he can't resist playing with the dogs and ends up taking them on wild adventures around the city. Jerry and George have to help him round up the dogs before they're taken away by animal control.\\n\\n5. \\\"The Carpool\\\": Jerry joins a carpool, but he's unhappy with the other passengers. He tries to get out of the carpool, but the others won't let him out. So, he decides to play a series of pranks to get rid of them.\"}", "question": "{\"type\": \"score\", \"instructions\": \"How helpful is `response` as an answer to `prompt`?\", \"criteria\": [\"Not useful or helpful at all; completely missed the essence of what the user wanted.\", \"Borderline unhelpful: mostly does not capture what the user was looking for, but still usable and helpful in a small way.\", \"Partially helpful: misses the overall goal of the user's query in some way; did not fully satisfy what the user was looking for.\", \"Mostly helpful and mainly aligned with what the user was looking for, but there i